\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Haar-preserving coordinate replacements}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Let #{G} be compact Hausdorff and equip #{G^m} with its
regular Haar probability. Consider a finite sequence of
coordinate replacements. In each step one current coordinate
#{x} is replaced by
##{y=A(z)x^\epsilon B(z),\qquad \epsilon\in\{1,-1\},}
where #{z} denotes all other current coordinates and #{A,B}
are continuous group-valued functions of #{z}. All coordinates
#{z} are left fixed by that step. Each replacement is an
invertible Haar-preserving homeomorphism.}

\p{Suppose some distinct output coordinates are retained and
never altered by subsequent steps. If their final values are
exactly the specified words of a
\vocabk{coarse path map}{fdip-00MO}, then its image reference
#{\rho} is product Haar on the retained links. Write the
composite change as #{\Phi:U\mapsto(u,v)}, where #{u} is retained
and #{v} contains the remaining coordinates. For compact
metrizable #{G}, a disintegration is explicitly
##{\kappa(u,\cdot)
 =\bigl(v\mapsto\Phi^{-1}(u,v)\bigr)_*\nu_v,}
with #{\nu_v} the product Haar probability of the unused
coordinates. In particular the image density is
##{f(u)=\int w(\Phi^{-1}(u,v))\,d\nu_v(v)}
for almost every #{u}, for every nonnegative integrable
fine weight #{w}.}

\proof{
\p{The inverse replacement is
##{x=\begin{cases}
 A(z)^{-1}yB(z)^{-1},&\epsilon=1,\\
 B(z)y^{-1}A(z),&\epsilon=-1.
 \end{cases}}
Both inverses are continuous. For fixed #{z}, translation
and inversion preserve Haar by \ref{fdip-007B}.
Test the transformed measure against a continuous function
on #{G^m}. Its iterated integral over #{x} and #{z} is
unchanged by this substitution. Iterated integration here
represents the regular product measure: equality holds on
continuous product functions and extends by uniform density.
Uniqueness of regular Borel measures from their continuous
integrals proves preservation of the entire Haar probability.
This argument does not identify the topological Borel
sigma-algebra with a product sigma-algebra in the
nonmetrizable case.}

\p{Composition preserves the same probability. Its retained
coordinate projection therefore has product Haar law, provided
that those coordinates are the stated path words. This
requires checking the final words after all replacements:
an occurrence once in a current pivot, with all other factors
independent of that pivot, is the condition used at each step.}

\p{For compact metrizable #{G}, finite Borel products agree
with product sigma-algebras. The displayed inverse coordinate
formula is then a Borel probability kernel. Fubini gives
##{\int F(U)\,d\nu_f(U)
 =\int\int F(\Phi^{-1}(u,v))\,d\nu_v(v)\,d\nu_c(u)}
for integrable #{F}; Tonelli gives the nonnegative version.
The kernel is supported on #{\mathcal B^{-1}\{u\}} for every
#{u}. Apply \ref{fdip-00MQ} with #{F=w} to obtain the density.}
}

\p{These substitutions include the rooted-tree changes in
\citet{Theorem 5.3, pp. 590–591}{driver1989ym2} and the
triangular lasso changes in
\citet{Corollary 2.4.9, pp. 102–103}{levy2010holonomy}.
The proof above applies to the stated compact-group class;
arbitrary repeated path words need not admit such a sequence.}
